PART 4 — PRIORITY ONE: IMPROVE COMBO ASSIST

This is the most important requested feature.

4.1 Current behavior

Alva currently supports Combo Assist for the human player.

When Attack 1 has finished and is on its own repeat cooldown, pressing Attack 1 again can request Attack 2 instead.

The same applies in reverse.

However, pressing the button corresponding to the attack currently playing is deliberately discarded.

It does not restart, cancel, or queue the attack.

This protects the current animation but can make repeated presses feel unresponsive.

4.2 Desired behavior

Implement one queued alternate attack when the player repeats the button responsible for an attack currently playing.

Examples:

Example A

* Press Attack 1.
* Attack 1 begins.
* Press Attack 1 again near the end of its animation.
* Attack 1 continues uninterrupted.
* Attack 1 finishes normally.
* Attack 2 becomes the next requested attack.
* If Attack 2 is eligible, it starts using the normal attack-start system.

Example B

* Press Attack 2.
* Attack 2 begins.
* Press Attack 2 again near its end.
* Attack 2 finishes.
* Attack 1 starts when legally available.

Example C

* Press Attack 1.
* Attack 1 finishes.
* Press Attack 1 while its repeat cooldown remains active.
* Existing Combo Assist behavior requests Attack 2.

Example C must continue to work exactly as before.

4.3 Exact queue rules

Use these initial rules:

Queue window: 0.20 seconds before the current attack’s natural completion.

This is a starting design value, not an already implemented setting.

At 60 Hz, 0.20 seconds is 12 simulation steps.

A repeated press within that window may queue the opposite attack.

A repeated press earlier than the window must not restart the move or cause an unintended future action.

There may be only one queued follow-up per running attack.

Repeated presses must not accumulate multiple future actions.

Holding an attack button must not continuously generate attacks.

Only actual press edges can create a queue.

The player must remain free to make other deliberate choices.

The queued follow-up must not make Attack 1 or Attack 2 automatically alternate forever.

4.4 Resolve the actual active attack

This is critical.

A physical Attack 1 press may have started Attack 2 because Combo Assist substituted the move.

Therefore, do not derive the next attack solely from the button’s name.

Resolve the follow-up from:

* The actual attack definition currently playing.
* Its source combat input.
* The fighter’s current loadout.
* Its ground/air variant.
* Its original attack instance.

If Attack 2 is currently playing because Attack 1 previously substituted it, repeating the responsible button should queue Attack 1.

Avoid implementing a system that accidentally queues Attack 2 again.

4.5 Preserve the complete current animation

A queued follow-up must never:

* Restart the current animation.
* Set its animation time back to zero.
* Cancel the current attack early.
* Skip startup frames.
* Remove active hitbox frames.
* Skip recovery.
* Trigger additional copies of a strike.
* Release a projectile earlier than intended.
* Bypass cooldown restrictions.

The current move must finish through its normal lifecycle.

The queued move may then begin on the earliest eligible simulation step.

Do not insert an unnecessary pause after completion.

Also do not give queued follow-ups artificial speed advantages over manually entered follow-ups.

4.6 Respect cooldowns

The existing cooldown system must remain authoritative.

The baseline ordinary move repeat cooldown is 0.5 seconds per move, beginning when that particular move ends, is canceled, or is interrupted.

It is not a universal 0.5-second delay between all attacks.

Exceptional cooldown classes are 3 seconds and 5 seconds under the existing policy.

Never shorten, bypass, overwrite, or prematurely expire these timers.

Before executing the queued attack, validate:

* The destination move exists.
* The destination move has its required art.
* The destination move is not cooling down.
* The destination move can start in its current location.
* Its airtime-use restrictions are satisfied.
* The fighter is not stunned, immobilized, or otherwise committed.
* No higher-priority player request has superseded it.

If the destination move cannot start at natural completion, discard that queued follow-up.

Do not restart the source attack as a fallback.

Do not keep the queued attack waiting until a long cooldown finishes.

4.7 Cancellation rules

Discard the queue when:

* The player is hit and the attack is interrupted.
* The source attack is manually canceled.
* A different combat action successfully replaces it.
* The player executes a higher-priority Jump, Dash, Shield, or Deflect request.
* A technique or summon takes control.
* The fighter is eliminated or leaves play.
* The fighter resets or respawns.
* Input becomes locked.
* The battle ends.
* Combo Assist is unavailable or disabled for that player.
* The source attack instance is no longer valid.
* A ground/air transition invalidates the queued destination.

A failed unrelated action should not necessarily erase a valid queue. Preserve a coherent priority policy.

Avoid stale queued attacks after interruptions.

4.8 Handle natural completion accurately

Do not implement completion detection using only the condition that combat.attack has become null.

An attack may disappear because of:

* Natural completion.
* A successful manual cancel.
* A hit interruption.
* Paralysis.
* Landing cancellation.
* A motion-specific early ending.
* Other state transitions.

Read the existing CombatState.endAttack() implementation and every caller.

Use reliable information about how the attack ended.

If necessary, introduce a small, deterministic completion signal or termination reason that distinguishes natural completion from interruption.

Do not infer completion from arbitrary animation state alone.

A landing cancel should not automatically qualify as a complete animation unless its defined behavior genuinely considers that attack finished.

Handle hitstop carefully: the queue should neither expire nor execute during frozen gameplay merely because wall-clock time passes.

4.9 Integrate with Combat Assist

A queued attack that becomes available should enter the same action-resolution path as a normal attack press.

For example, if Combo Assist queues Attack 2 and Combat Assist is enabled, the destination move may use Combat Assist’s existing approach if appropriate.

Do not create a separate, simplified attack launcher.

Use the same eligibility and action-start code.

The result must work with:

* Combat Assist on / Combo Assist on.
* Combat Assist off / Combo Assist on.
* Combat Assist on / Combo Assist off.
* Both assists off.

The assists must remain independent settings.

4.10 Preserve manual control

An explicit Attack 1 → Attack 2 press should retain Alva’s existing legal cancellation behavior.

The new repeat-button queue is distinct from deliberately pressing a different attack button.

Do not replace deliberate manual cancellation with automatic delayed inputs.

Advanced players should retain the ability to perform their intended cancels.

The queue must improve accessibility, not take away player control.

