PART 5 — PRIORITY TWO: IMPROVE INPUT BUFFERING

5.1 Existing input buffer

Alva currently uses a 0.15-second combat input buffer.

An ordinary attack pressed too early can be remembered until it becomes available.

At 60 Hz, this is approximately nine simulation frames.

This is useful, but may be slightly unforgiving for beginners.

5.2 Proposed adjustment

Evaluate increasing the ordinary attack buffer from 0.15 seconds to 0.20 seconds.

Treat 0.20 seconds as the preferred initial candidate.

Evaluate 0.25 seconds only if reproducible test scenarios show that 0.20 remains unnecessarily strict.

Do not increase the buffer just to make the numerical value larger.

The change should measurably improve intended input acceptance without causing unwanted delayed attacks.

5.3 Separate attack buffering from combo queuing

The ordinary attack buffer and Combo Assist queue are different mechanisms.

Ordinary input buffer: Keeps a legal attack intention that cannot start immediately.

Combo Assist queue: Holds one alternate follow-up until the current attack animation finishes naturally.

Do not put both mechanisms into the same ambiguous queue without clearly defined ownership.

They must not:

* Execute the same input twice.
* Compete unpredictably.
* Restart attacks.
* Execute stale requests.
* Override a newer valid input.
* Violate action priority.
* Produce repeated attacks from held buttons.

5.4 Important existing dependency

Inspect BASE_FIGHTER_MOVEMENT.attackBuffer carefully.

The current value is also involved in Dash buffering.

If increasing it would unintentionally change Dash behavior, consider separating ordinary attack-buffer duration from Dash-buffer duration using universal configuration.

Do not silently change both systems when only ordinary attack forgiveness was requested.

Keep the design data-driven, universal, and consistent with the repository’s movement validation.

5.5 Preserve input semantics

Preserve:

* Latest valid buffered attack taking priority.
* Correct ordering with buffered jumps.
* Buffered presses during hitstop not aging through the freeze.
* Ground/air input context.
* Attacks with missing sprites being refused.
* Specials and techniques retaining their current buffering restrictions.
* Inputs from before a received hit being cleared.
* Reserved or unmapped controls never creating phantom requests.
* Same-step precedence.
* Device-independent behavior.

A queued or buffered attack must feel intentional rather than surprising.

