PART 6 — PRIORITY THREE: EXAMINE EARLY ATTACK CANCELLATION

6.1 The problem

Alva allows the human player to deliberately cancel an ongoing attack with another eligible request.

This can happen during startup, active frames, or recovery.

That gives the player substantial control and makes the game responsive.

However, a beginner may press a second attack before the first attack’s hitbox becomes active.

The second attack can then replace the first one before it has even struck.

This can make fighting feel confusing.

A player may believe their attack failed when the real problem is that they accidentally canceled its startup.

6.2 Do not remove manual cancellation

Alva’s current player-cancellation system is intentional and explicitly documented.

Do not globally disable startup cancellation or require every attack to finish before a different attack can start.

That would be a major regression.

Instead:

1. Study how often early attack cancellation can occur under realistic input sequences.
2. Measure whether basic beginners’ input patterns lose intended hitboxes.
3. Identify the precise cancellation scenarios responsible.
4. Determine whether Combo Assist queuing solves the most common case.
5. Preserve explicit different-button cancellation unless a narrowly scoped improvement is demonstrably better.

6.3 Avoid artificial restrictions

Do not add a universal recovery lockout between different moves.

Do not confuse per-move repeat cooldowns with cancellation.

Do not add arbitrary delays to manual attack switching.

Do not automatically finish a move that an experienced player deliberately canceled.

The preferred approach is to first solve repeated-button behavior through the new Combo Assist queue and improve player feedback.

Only propose a further startup-cancellation rule if measurable evidence demonstrates a remaining significant problem.

Any such additional rule must be explicitly documented and carefully scoped, preferably to a player-assist context instead of replacing the shared combat contract.

