PART 12 — SPECIAL CASES AND EDGE CONDITIONS

The upgraded system must handle the following correctly.

12.1 Multihit attacks

A multihit attack must complete all its intended strikes when left uninterrupted.

A queued combo must not start after the first strike merely because the first hit connected.

It starts after the source animation naturally completes.

Do not accidentally truncate #0002’s Rapid Kicks or #0004’s Dance Combo.

12.2 Projectiles

An existing projectile release must occur at its authored timing.

A queue must not duplicate projectile spawning.

A projectile already released may continue independently when the fighter moves on.

12.3 Aerial attacks

Aerial Attack 1 and Attack 2 may differ from their ground versions.

Respect:

* Ground/air mapping.
* Air-use limits.
* Free fall.
* Landing.
* Landing cancellation.
* Motion-specific attack completion.
* Remaining aerial movement resources.

Never start a ground-only move in the air because a queued action was stored earlier.

Do not silently turn an intended aerial follow-up into a different ground move.

12.4 Motion attacks

Review interactions with:

* Homing attacks.
* Bounce attacks.
* Slams.
* Rolls.
* Rising attacks.
* Hovering attacks.
* Attacks with motion-driven early termination.

Do not override these mechanics with generic animation-completion assumptions.

12.5 Simultaneous input

Define deterministic behavior for:

* Two attack buttons pressed in the same simulation step.
* Attack and Dash on the same step.
* Attack and Jump on the same step.
* Attack and Shield on the same step.
* A repeated attack press on the exact step an animation ends.
* A buffered request and queued follow-up becoming available together.
* A hit occurring on a frame that also ends an attack.

Preserve Alva’s established action priority whenever possible.

One request must not generate two unintended actions.

12.6 Hitstop and stun

Check what happens when a player repeats a button:

* During startup.
* During active frames.
* During recovery.
* During hitstop.
* Just before taking a hit.
* During hitstun.
* Immediately after recovering from stun.

Do not allow a previously queued attack to emerge after an interruption that should have cleared it.

12.7 Pausing, switching, and restarting

Queues and buffers must not produce unexpected attacks after:

* Pausing or resuming.
* Returning to Home.
* Changing the controlled fighter.
* Starting a new match.
* Restarting Practice Ground.
* Respawning.
* Switching controller devices.
* Losing focus and having inputs reset.
* A result or round-ending input lock.

