PART 14 — MEASURE WHETHER FIGHTING ACTUALLY IMPROVES

A feature being implemented does not automatically mean combat feels better.

Create reproducible before-and-after scenarios.

Where practical, measure:

Input responsiveness

* Press-to-attack acceptance rate.
* Number of valid early presses lost.
* Delay between legal attack availability and actual startup.
* Number of unintended repeated actions.
* Number of duplicate actions produced by a single press.

Combo Assist

* Percentage of correctly queued follow-ups.
* Number of accidental animation restarts.
* Number of stale attacks after interruption.
* Number of queued moves that bypassed cooldowns (must be zero).
* Number of unintended auto-chains (must be zero).

Attack spacing

* Contact success at clearly defined distances.
* Contact failure outside valid range.
* Differences between intended sprite contact and hitbox contact.
* Whether Combat Assist improves short-range approach reliability.

Cancellation

* Number of attacks canceled before their active frames in standardized novice-style input sequences.
* Whether repeated-button queuing reduces this specific problem.
* Whether deliberate different-button cancellations remain immediately responsive.

Use the tests and available simulation harness rather than fabricating statistics.

A useful quality target is to make intended simple two-button sequences more reliable without reducing the importance of spacing, timing, Shield use, movement, and opponent reactions.

Do not guarantee that every attack hits.

