PART 15 — IMPLEMENTATION ARCHITECTURE

Implement the work in an organized manner.

15.1 Separate responsibilities

The preferred conceptual ownership is:

combo-assist.js

Owns the general rules for identifying alternate attacks.

Keep these rules generic and independent of specific fighters.

fighter.js

Owns transient player-input state, queuing, priority, invalidation, and integration with the existing attack-start pipeline.

combat-state.js

Owns attack state and lifecycle.

If reliable natural-completion reporting is needed, implement it here without confusing normal completion with manual cancellation.

movement.js

Owns universal buffering values when relevant.

Avoid putting fighter-specific responsiveness values in character definitions.

move-status.js

Provides read-only presentation state.

It should not own combat input or queues.

UI modules

Display confirmed combat states without changing them.

15.2 Design a safe queue object

Use a single pending-combo object rather than an unbounded array.

Its state should contain enough information to determine:

* Which attack instance created it.
* Which input created it.
* Which destination action was selected.
* Whether it belongs to ground or air.
* When it was requested in simulation time.
* Whether its source attack remains valid.

Store the destination as the resolved action at queue time.

Do not repeatedly reapply Combo Assist and accidentally change its destination.

Do not use a timer that executes actions independently of Fighter.update().

15.3 Avoid race conditions

Do not mix old and new inputs in an ambiguous order.

Review exactly where CombatState.update(dt) ends an attack.

Natural completion and queue execution should fit into the existing fixed-step action ordering.

Do not execute a queued action if a newer valid request already took priority.

Never perform multiple successful move starts on one simulation step.

15.4 Do not duplicate attack-start logic

Queued attacks should use existing eligibility functions and tryAction() or an appropriately refactored common action-start pathway.

Do not implement a second function that ignores restrictions.

A future fighter with a new attack type should work through the same shared system.

15.5 Keep implementation modest

Prefer small, well-factored changes over a giant rewrite of Fighter.update().

Add helper functions where doing so meaningfully clarifies behavior.

Avoid introducing an unnecessarily complicated state machine for a one-action queue.

Every new state variable must have a clearly defined lifecycle.

