PART 19 — ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The implementation is successful when all of the following are true.

Combo Assist

A human player with Combo Assist enabled can press Attack 1, then repeat the responsible attack button near the end of the animation, and receive a legal Attack 2 follow-up after Attack 1 naturally completes.

The same works from Attack 2 to Attack 1.

The original attack never restarts, skips frames, or loses its strike because of that repeated press.

No move ignores its cooldown.

No attack starts from a stale queue after interruption.

Manual different-button cancellations still work.

Responsiveness

Ordinary attack presses made slightly early are handled more forgivingly.

New buffering does not produce unexpected delayed attacks.

Attack, Jump, Shield, and Dash priorities remain coherent.

Attack consistency

The most common basic attacks behave consistently with their sprite animations, hitboxes, facing, and positioning.

Any hitbox corrections are targeted and tested.

Assists

Combo Assist and Combat Assist work independently or together.

Both continue to function with every playable fighter.

Both preserve their intended optional nature.

Combat depth

Skilled players retain deliberate cancellations, spacing, movement options, counterplay, and timing advantages.

The game does not become an automatic attack sequence.

CPU

CPU behavior and difficulty remain intentionally unchanged.

Architecture

The fixed-step simulation remains deterministic.

Universal systems remain universal.

Input methods stay consistent.

The repository’s technical architecture remains intact.

Quality

All relevant tests pass, documentation matches implementation, and the finished changes are explained.

