FINAL PRODUCT VISION

Alva should be easy to begin playing but rewarding to master.

A beginner should be able to press basic attack buttons and obtain understandable, predictable actions.

An experienced player should still gain an advantage from deliberate timing, accurate spacing, movement, Shield usage, hit confirmation, and manual cancellation.

An attack should not feel unresponsive merely because a reasonable input occurred a few frames early.

A repeated attack press should not unexpectedly restart and cancel an active animation.

Combo Assist should understand the player’s intended sequence and perform a legal follow-up without bypassing the existing attack lifecycle.

Combat Assist should make short-range melee engagement more consistent without guaranteeing hits or invalidating positioning.

Animation, hitbox, and collision behavior should agree as closely as practical.

Cooldowns should remain meaningful but should not be confused with general attack delays.

Above all, the system should make the player feel that their character responds reliably to their intentions while remaining subject to fair and understandable combat rules.

The goal is not to simplify Alva’s mechanics.

The goal is to make those mechanics communicate and cooperate better.

Implement a precise, tested, well-documented combat-feel improvement using Alva’s existing architecture. Prioritize Combo Assist queued follow-ups, input responsiveness, attack consistency, and player feedback. Preserve advanced mechanics and leave CPU difficulty untouched.

Begin by inspecting the latest repository state, establishing a passing baseline, and tracing the existing combat lifecycle. Then implement the improvements in the order specified above, validate them against every currently playable fighter, and provide the requested engineering report.
